Renal Cell Carcinoma History
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Macroscopic haematuria, Dull flank pain, Palpable abdominal mass, Enhancing renal mass on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-040
Reason for Visit: Sudden passage of heavy blood clots in the urine, severe flank pain, and profound systemic exhaustion requiring immediate evaluation for haemorrhage or obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported gross painless haematuria rapidly progressing to the passage of large clots, accompanied by a heavy flank ache and significant systemic decline. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to assess haemodynamic stability, explicitly rule out acute clot retention obstructing the bladder, and evaluate a newly noted palpable flank mass. Deferring care risked occult haemorrhagic shock or acute urinary obstruction requiring emergency catheterization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My urine is entirely dark red with heavy clots, I have a constant heavy ache in my side, and I have lost so much weight I barely recognize myself.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with the classic triad of macroscopic haematuria, persistent dull flank pain, and a palpable unilateral abdominal mass. Tonight, the haematuria acutely worsened, with the passage of large, thick clots causing severe discomfort. They report unexplained, unintentional significant weight loss of twelve kilograms over three months, accompanied by systemic fatigue, night sweats, and generalized malaise. They note an unexplained low-grade fever over the past week, not associated with typical infectious symptoms. The patient has a 30-pack-year history of heavy smoking.
Functional Status: Weak and easily fatigued, currently resting in a recliner due to the dull flank pain and systemic exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the visible blood loss; significant risk of acute urinary obstruction from clot retention.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.8C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Cachectic, pale, appearing chronically ill and visibly exhausted.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Deep palpation reveals a firm, palpable, non-tender unilateral mass in the right flank, distinct from the liver edge. Back: Dull, constant right-sided flank ache, but no sharp costovertebral angle tenderness to suggest acute infection. Genitourinary: Point-of-care urinalysis confirms florid macroscopic haematuria. A new-onset left-sided varicocele is noted. Cardiovascular: Pallor secondary to chronic disease anaemia is evident in the conjunctivae. Expected investigation results upon urgent imaging will reveal a solid, heterogeneous, and contrast-enhancing renal mass on a dedicated multiphase abdominal CT scan.
Environmental Observations: Ashtrays present; patient appears to be living in a state of self-neglect due to profound fatigue.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications known to cause bleeding (e.g., anticoagulants).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Renal Cell Carcinoma - Suspected (Acute Exacerbation with Clot Haematuria)
Differentials:

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain being a dull, constant ache rather than severe, colicky radiation, and the highly specific presence of a palpable mass and profound cachexia.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the chronicity of the symptoms, the massive weight loss, and the absence of classic infective markers (nitrites) on urinalysis, despite the paraneoplastic fever.

Polycystic Kidney Disease - Ruled out/unlikely as the palpable mass is strictly unilateral, and there is no known family history of the disorder.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The triad of a palpable flank mass, gross macroscopic haematuria, and a dull flank ache in a cachectic patient with a heavy smoking history is highly suspicious for advanced Renal Cell Carcinoma. The acute passage of large clots raised the immediate threat of acute urinary obstruction or severe haemorrhagic decompensation. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to verify the patient's haemodynamic stability, palpate the abdomen to confirm the presence of the mass rather than an overdistended obstructed bladder, and initiate immediate fast-tracked oncological investigations. Delaying this assessment would have left a highly vulnerable patient at risk of silent exsanguination or agonizing clot retention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Generated an urgent, fast-tracked referral for a comprehensive CT Abdomen/Pelvis with contrast. Ordered urgent morning bloods including Full Blood Count (to check for severe anaemia or paraneoplastic erythrocytosis), UECs, and Calcium levels. Directed the patient to maintain a high fluid intake to mechanically flush the bladder and prevent clot retention.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure the continuous flushing of the renal tract, the patient is instructed to drink a large glass of water every time they pass a blood clot. The visual feedback of the subsequent urine appearing lighter and less clot-heavy will provide strong positive reinforcement, cementing the habit of maintaining a high-volume diuresis to protect their bladder out-flow tract.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 48 hours to review the urgent CT imaging and coordinate urgent urology and oncology referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 is required if the patient feels profoundly dizzy or faints when standing up, or if they become completely unable to pass urine due to a clot blockage.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical findings and weight loss are highly concerning for a significant mass in the kidney; the urgent CT scan is required to definitively diagnose the cause.
Clinical Course: The bleeding may continue intermittently. It is critical to keep the urine dilute to prevent clots from forming a physical blockage in the bladder.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you cannot pee at all because of a blood clot, or if you feel lightheaded, sweaty, and faint, you must go directly to the Emergency Department as you may be losing too much blood.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A large, abnormal mass has grown in your right kidney, which is bleeding into your urine system and causing your severe weight loss and fatigue. We must urgently image this mass to determine exactly what it is. The most important thing tonight is to prevent those blood clots from blocking your bladder.
Actionable Steps: Drink at least three liters of water over the next 24 hours. Attend the urgent blood test and CT scan immediately tomorrow morning. Do not take any aspirin or ibuprofen.

